<div class="grid grid-cols-1 gap-5 sm:grid-cols-3">
  <%# The one heading in the app that titles a thing that happened rather than labelling a
      screen you are about to act on, which is what makes it a display surface under #200's
      rule. It is still a page title and still obeys #128's shape -- an h1 at text-3xl,
      semibold, in the title colour -- so this is both, and `serif` outranks the heading
      rule on specificity to decide which face wins. %>
  <h1 class="serif text-3xl font-semibold col-span-2 text-gray-900"><%= @workout[:date].strftime('%A %B %-d, %Y') %></h1>
  <div class="flex flex-col gap-2">
    <%# Both of these used to hover to gray-500, which on a control means disabled. It kept
        surviving review because white on gray-500 is 4.83:1 and white on lime-500 is 1.98:1,
        so the broken state was the more legible one. Each colour steps one down its own scale
        now, sky included -- a secondary that turns primary under the pointer is no hierarchy. %>
    <a href="/workouts/<%= @workout[:id] %>/session" type="button" class="<%= button_style %> inline-flex items-center gap-x-1.5 hover:bg-sky-900 bg-sky-800 px-2.5 py-1.5 text-sm font-semibold text-white">
      <svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="currentColor" class="w-6 h-6">
        <path fill-rule="evenodd" d="M2.25 12c0-5.385 4.365-9.75 9.75-9.75s9.75 4.365 9.75 9.75-4.365 9.75-9.75 9.75S2.25 17.385 2.25 12zm14.024-.983a1.125 1.125 0 010 1.966l-5.603 3.113A1.125 1.125 0 019 15.113V8.887c0-.857.921-1.4 1.671-.983l5.603 3.113z" clip-rule="evenodd" />
      </svg>
      Run session
    </a>
    <a href="/workouts/<%= @workout[:id] %>/sets/new" type="button" class="<%= button_style %> inline-flex items-center gap-x-1.5 hover:bg-lime-600 bg-lime-500 px-2.5 py-1.5 text-sm font-semibold text-gray-900">
      <svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="currentColor" class="w-6 h-6">
        <path fill-rule="evenodd" d="M12 2.25c-5.385 0-9.75 4.365-9.75 9.75s4.365 9.75 9.75 9.75 9.75-4.365 9.75-9.75S17.385 2.25 12 2.25zM12.75 9a.75.75 0 00-1.5 0v2.25H9a.75.75 0 000 1.5h2.25V15a.75.75 0 001.5 0v-2.25H15a.75.75 0 000-1.5h-2.25V9z" clip-rule="evenodd" />
      </svg>
      Add new set
    </a>
  </div>
</div>

<%- if provenance(@workout) %>
  <p class="mt-2 text-xs text-gray-500"><%= provenance(@workout) %></p>
<%- end %>

<%# What the lifter said about the day. #310, and it sits above the numbers rather than under
    them because it is the thing that explains them: an RPE of 9 and a long turnaround both
    read differently once "slept badly" is on the page.

    whitespace-pre-line keeps the line breaks somebody put between two thoughts while still
    wrapping a long line on a phone, which is the one thing pre would not do -- the same
    treatment views/exercises/show.erb gives a movement's note, and for the same reason. %>
<%- if @workout.note %>
  <p class="mt-3 whitespace-pre-line text-sm text-gray-700"><%= @workout.note %></p>
<%- end %>

<%# What went well, named specifically. #410, the second half.
    Above the numbers and above the diagnostics, which is the ordering #410 asks for in so
    many words: a session that ran long and still moved a training max is a good session, and
    what gets read first has to say so. The diagnostics are #409's and sit below the fold.

    Darker than the grey beneath it and in the lime the app uses for work that was done, so
    the two blocks read as different kinds of statement rather than as one long paragraph of
    small print. A list rather than a sentence, because these are separate facts about
    separate things and running them together would make the second one look like a
    qualification of the first.

    Nothing at all where there is nothing to say, which is the property that keeps it worth
    reading. A summary that appears every time, saying something every time, is a summary
    nobody reads by the third week -- so a session where nothing was beaten, no max moved and
    nothing held gets no block here, and the record reads exactly as it did before. %>
<%- unless session_summary.empty? %>
  <ul class="mt-3 space-y-1">
    <%- session_summary.each do |line| %>
      <li class="text-sm font-medium text-lime-800"><%= line %></li>
    <%- end %>
  </ul>
<%- end %>

<%# How long it took, once it is over. #281.
    The record is where a measured session is read back, and it is the only place these
    numbers are worth keeping still -- on the session screen they are a clock, here they are
    a fact about a day that is finished.

    How long from the first set to the last (or to the moment the lifter said they were
    done); what a normal turnaround between sets was; and how many turnarounds were thrown
    out for being too long to be training. That last one earns its place: "typical 2m" over
    a session with four discarded gaps is a different session from one with none, and
    without the count nobody can tell which they are reading.

    Since #318 there are two lengths rather than one, and the second only appears when it
    differs. A session with no long gaps has an active time equal to its span, and printing
    "48m active - 48m elapsed" would be saying the same thing twice on a line that is
    already three clauses long. Where they do differ -- a session logged either side of
    midnight -- both are shown, because the active figure rests on a guess about what counts
    as training and the raw span beside it is what keeps that guess from destroying
    anything.

    "Turnaround" and not "rest", throughout. One tap per set measures the gap between two
    taps, which is the rest plus the working time of the set that ended it -- see
    lib/tectonic/timing.rb. Calling it rest would be naming a number the app cannot see.

    Nothing at all on a session with no stamps, which is every session logged before #281
    and every one written but not yet trained. An empty line saying "0m" would be a claim
    about training that never happened. %>
<%- if @timing[:overall] %>
  <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-gray-500">
    <%# The app's own arithmetic, matched watch or not. #571.

        This line used to defer, and the deferral was the substance of #520: *"where the two
        disagree about how long a session took, the watch is right and the app should say so
        rather than quietly keeping its own figure."* #528 implemented it literally -- the
        watch's span printed first and labelled as measured, the tap-derived figure kept
        beside it and hedged as "from your taps" so that nobody could confuse the two.

        The rule was right about instruments in general and wrong about this instrument. On
        the reporting account's own data the watch starts late and runs long: it called a
        session 6:13 to 7:18 that ran 6:07 to 6:53, twenty-five minutes of its recording
        falling after the last completed set, and on the following morning it bracketed a
        shorter, later window than the session it was supposedly measuring. Both of those
        rows carry `attrib = 7` and came back with no `effduration`, which is what an
        activity the watch *detected* looks like rather than one somebody deliberately
        started on it. So deferring did not put a real number in place of an estimate; it
        redisplayed a 46 minute session as an hour, over the top of the right answer. #571
        carries the numbers.

        What the taps measure is when a set was actually marked done. That is an inference
        from somebody touching a phone, and #520 was right that it is an inference -- but it
        is an inference about the lifting, and on this evidence a closer one than the watch
        manages. So the figure here is the app's, always, and it means the same thing on
        every session in a week read back.

        **"from your taps" went with it.** It was never a hedge about accuracy: it was there
        because a second number stood beside this one and a reader was owed the name of the
        instrument each came from. With nothing competing, it only makes a plain measurement
        sound unsure of itself, and it would be the one clause on the page apologising for
        the number the page is most confident about. The answer #528 was reaching for -- a
        reader can always tell which instrument a number came from -- is kept by putting what
        only the watch knows in views/workouts/_withings_match.erb and labelling all of it as
        the watch's, rather than by labelling this. %>
    <%- if @timing[:discarded].positive? %>
      <%= Timing.phrase(@timing[:active]) %> active
      <span aria-hidden="true">&middot;</span>
      <%= Timing.phrase(@timing[:overall]) %> elapsed<%= ' so far' if @timing[:overall_basis] == :in_progress %>
    <%- else %>
      <%= Timing.phrase(@timing[:overall]) %><%= ' so far' if @timing[:overall_basis] == :in_progress %>
    <%- end %>
    <%- if @timing[:typical_turnaround] %>
      <span aria-hidden="true">&middot;</span>
      typically <%= Timing.phrase(@timing[:typical_turnaround]) %> between sets
    <%- end %>
    <%- if @timing[:discarded].positive? %>
      <span aria-hidden="true">&middot;</span>
      <%= @timing[:discarded] %> long <%= @timing[:discarded] == 1 ? 'gap' : 'gaps' %> not counted
    <%- end %>
  </p>
  <%# When it started and when it ended. #397.

      A second line rather than a fifth clause on the one above, which is already three
      clauses long on a phone. It answers a different question too: the line above is how
      long the session took, this is where it sat in the day -- which is what somebody
      reading back a week of training actually wants, and is the difference between "48m"
      and "48m, and it was the six o'clock session".

      Only on a session that has ended. While one is still running the finish time does not
      exist yet, and the line above already says "so far" about it; printing a start alone
      under that reads as though the session were over. The stamps themselves are the same
      two `overall` has always subtracted -- this stops throwing the ends away. %>
  <%# The session's own two ends, matched watch or not. #571.

      These deferred too, and had to while the line above did: a page reporting a 49 minute
      session running from 18:02 to 18:55 leaves a reader working out which half to believe,
      so #528 took both halves from the same instrument and the watch's ends were the ones
      its span was the difference of. That constraint is intact and satisfied the other way
      round -- the length above is the difference of these two, because both are the taps.

      **And the guard on a session still running comes back.** #528 let a matched session
      past `overall_basis == :finished`, reasoning that the watch has a real measured end
      whether or not anybody tapped finish, so there was nothing to withhold. That reasoning
      was about the watch's end and this is not the watch's end. The app's end of a session
      nobody has finished does not exist yet -- the line above says "so far" about exactly
      that -- and printing a start underneath it would read as though the session were over,
      which is the whole of what this guard has always been for.

      The watch's own two ends are not lost with the deferral. They keep a line of their own
      in views/workouts/_withings_match.erb, named there as the watch's recording, which is
      where a lifter wondering why their watch says an hour can find the answer without it
      ever being the figure labelled as the session. %>
  <%- if @timing[:started_at] && @timing[:overall_basis] == :finished %>
    <p class="mt-1 text-sm text-gray-500">
      <%== render('workouts/_clock_time', locals: { at: @timing[:started_at] }) %>
      to
      <%== render('workouts/_clock_time', locals: { at: @timing[:ended_at] }) %>
    </p>
  <%- end %>
  <%# Why it ran the way it did. #409.

      Three causes look identical in a log -- the rests ran long, more was written than the
      time allowed, or the lifter started late -- and two of them are already distinguishable
      from what is stored. The third is not modelled and never will be: only the lifter knows
      what late means, and a wrong guess about it reads as an accusation.

      **Below the fold, which #409 is explicit about.** A session that ran long and still
      moved a training max is a good session, and the ordering has to say so -- so this sits
      under how long it took and under when it was, in the same grey as both, rather than at
      the top where it would be the first thing read about a session that went well.

      Silent where there is nothing to explain. A session lifted exactly as written gets no
      line at all, rather than a line saying that everything was fine in three clauses --
      which is the difference between a diagnosis and a scolding. %>
  <%- if session_diagnosis&.anything? %>
    <p class="mt-1 text-sm text-gray-500"><%= Tectonic::SessionDiagnosis.sentence(session_diagnosis) %></p>
  <%- end %>
<%- end %>

<%# What the watch has to say, and the question about whether it is about this session.
    #520. Outside the timing block above deliberately: a session with no stamps has no
    length to print and the block draws nothing for it, but it can still be a session an
    account with a watch wants to stop being asked about. %>
<%== render('workouts/_withings_match') %>

<%# The clock times above, put into the reader's own timezone. #397.

    Rewritten here rather than sent ready-made, because the server has no idea where anybody
    is -- see the note in _clock_time.erb and #349. toLocaleTimeString asks the browser, which
    does know, and formats it the way that reader's locale writes a time.

    Every [data-local] on the page rather than two elements by id, so a third time added later
    needs nothing here. Degrades to the labelled UTC reading the server wrote, which is why
    that fallback says UTC rather than looking local. %>
<script>
  (function () {
    var times = document.querySelectorAll('time[data-local]');
    for (var i = 0; i < times.length; i++) {
      var at = parseInt(times[i].getAttribute('data-local'), 10);
      if (!at) { continue; }
      times[i].textContent =
        new Date(at * 1000).toLocaleTimeString([], { hour: 'numeric', minute: '2-digit' });
    }
  })();
</script>

<%# One column on a phone. grid-cols-2 was unprefixed, so it applied at every width and
    put a 392px table inside a 161px card -- and the card's overflow-hidden ate the rest
    silently, with no page-level scrollbar to show anything was missing. %>
<dl class="mt-5 grid grid-cols-1 gap-5 sm:grid-cols-2 lg:grid-cols-3">
  <%- @lifts.each do |exercise_id, sets| %>
    <%# The row itself, rather than the id twice over: the card names the movement and now
        draws it too. It comes out of the hash the route loaded rather than from Exercise[],
        which was a query per card -- see #234. An instance
        variable rather than a local, as in views/sets/_exercise_options.erb, because
        erb_lint hands each tag to rubocop as a snippet of its own and a local assigned in
        one tag and read in the next is a useless assignment to every cop that sees it. %>
    <%- @exercise = @exercises[exercise_id] %>
    <div class="relative overflow-hidden rounded-xl bg-white px-4 pt-5 shadow sm:px-6 sm:pt-6">
      <dt class="inline-flex">
        <div class="absolute rounded-md bg-lime-500 p-2">
          <%# The movement's own icon, not one figure for all of them. The icons the app
              ships are black; text-white sat here for the white-filled figure they replace
              and never reached inside the img in any case, CSS colour not crossing into a
              document loaded through src. Black on lime-500 is 10.63:1 where white on it is
              1.98:1, which is the arithmetic that took white off the session buttons. %>
          <img src="<%= @exercise.icon[:src] %>" alt="<%= @exercise.icon[:alt] %>" class="h-12 w-12">
        </div>
        <%# `brand` because this card title is an anchor and not a heading, so it has to
            ask. It read in the display face before only because `text-xl` happened to be
            in the font rule, which meant the day it became `sm:text-xl` the face would
            have gone with nobody touching a font. %>
        <a href="/exercises/<%= exercise_id %>" class="brand ml-20 md:ml-20 text-xl sm:text-4xl text-gray-500 hover:text-lime-500">
          <%= @exercise.name %>
        </a>
      </dt>
      <%# The columns do not wrap, so a narrow card still needs somewhere for them to go:
          scrolling reaches them, where the card's own overflow-hidden only hid them. %>
      <div class="mt-8 overflow-x-auto">
      <table class="min-w-full divide-y divide-gray-100">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col" class="py-3.5 pl-4 pr-3 text-left text-xs font-semibold sm:pl-0">Weight</th>
            <th scope="col" class="px-3 py-3.5 text-left text-xs font-semibold">Reps</th>
            <th scope="col" class="px-3 py-3.5 text-left text-xs font-semibold">Warmup?</th>
            <th scope="col" class="px-3 py-3.5 text-left text-xs font-semibold">Completed?</th>
            <th scope="col" class="relative py-3.5 pl-3 pr-4 sm:pr-0">
              <span class="sr-only">Edit</span>
            </th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody class="divide-y divide-gray-100">
          <%- sets.each do | set | %>
              <tr>
                <td class="whitespace-nowrap px-3 py-4">
                  <a href="/workouts/<%= @workout[:id] %>/sets/<%= set[:id] %>">
                    <%= weight_label(set[:weight]) || 'Bodyweight' %>
                  </a>
                </td>
                <td class="whitespace-nowrap px-3 py-4"><%= set[:reps] %></td>
                <%# Two columns headed with a question, so the answer is a box that is
                    ticked or not. "Warmup set" against "No" answered two different
                    questions in the same column, and a word has to be read where a
                    column of boxes is scanned. %>
                <td class="whitespace-nowrap px-3 py-4"><%== ticked(set[:is_warmup], 'warmup') %></td>
                <td class="whitespace-nowrap px-3 py-4"><%== ticked(set[:is_completed], 'completed') %></td>
                <%# lime-700, the same link colour as everywhere else. This was lime-400
                    hovering to lime-300 -- 1.51:1 on white going to 1.31:1 -- so the only
                    way to correct a set from the workout record faded as you reached for
                    it. Hover now darkens to lime-800 rather than lightening. %>
                <td class="relative whitespace-nowrap py-4 pl-3 pr-4 text-right text-sm font-medium sm:pr-0">
                  <a href="/workouts/<%= @workout[:id] %>/sets/<%= set[:id] %>/edit" class="text-lime-700 hover:text-lime-800">Edit</a>
                </td>
              </tr>
          <%- end %>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      </div>
    </div>
  <%- end %>
</dl>
